 "standard_text": "Use algebraic methods to solve linear equations in one variable (including all forms that require rearrangement); find approximate solutions to contextual problems from given graphs of a variety of functions.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 9 simultaneous equations worksheets: solve by elimination (adding, subtracting, multiplying first) and by substitution, then word problems. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Eliminate one letter, find the other, then substitute back.",
 "key_sub": "Same signs: subtract. Different signs: add. Always check in both equations.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p><b>Simultaneous equations</b> are two equations that are true at the same time. The solution is the one pair of values (<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>) that works in <b>both</b>. It is where the two lines cross.</p><p><b>Elimination:</b> make one letter have the same number in front in both equations. If the signs are <b>different, add</b>. If the signs are the <b>same, subtract</b>. <b>Substitution:</b> if one equation says <i>y</i> = …, put that into the other equation.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve 3<i>x</i> + <i>y</i> = 11 and <i>x</i> − <i>y</i> = 1.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Signs differ, so add: 4<i>x</i> = 12, <i>x</i> = 3.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3 − <i>y</i> = 1, so <i>y</i> = 2.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 3, <i>y</i> = 2</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve 2<i>x</i> + 3<i>y</i> = 12 and <i>x</i> + <i>y</i> = 5.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Multiply the second by 2: 2<i>x</i> + 2<i>y</i> = 10.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Subtract: <i>y</i> = 2, then <i>x</i> = 3.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 3, <i>y</i> = 2</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve <i>y</i> = 2<i>x</i> and <i>x</i> + <i>y</i> = 9.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>x</i> + 2<i>x</i> = 9, so <i>x</i> = 3.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>y</i> = 2 × 3 = 6.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 3, <i>y</i> = 6</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Adding when you should subtract. Same signs: subtract. Different signs: add.</li><li>Multiplying only one side of an equation. Multiply every term.</li><li>Finding <i>x</i> and stopping. You must find <i>y</i> as well.</li><li>Swapping the values of <i>x</i> and <i>y</i>. Check your answer in both equations.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What are simultaneous equations?",
   "a": "Two equations with two unknowns that are both true at once. The solution is the pair of values that works in both."
  },
  {
   "q": "Do I add or subtract the equations?",
   "a": "Look at the letter you want to remove. If its signs are the same, subtract. If they are different, add."
  },
  {
   "q": "When should I use substitution?",
   "a": "When one equation already says y = … or x = …. Put that expression into the other equation."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do simultaneous equations link to graphs?",
   "a": "Each equation is a straight line. The solution is the point where the two lines cross."
  }
 ],
